WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.050
Harvest-now, decrypt-later planning requires a basic understanding of cryptographic roles.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:09.160
Public-key cryptography helps systems establish trust or shared secrets without already

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.195
having a shared secret.

00:00:10.195 --> 00:00:15.595
It appears in certificates, key exchange, digital signatures, TLS handshakes, VPNs, SSH,

00:00:15.595 --> 00:00:18.030
secure email, code signing, and identity systems.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:22.200
Some widely used public-key algorithms are the main focus of quantum-related migration

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.760
planning because their security assumptions may not hold against sufficiently capable

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:26.660
quantum computers.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.740
Symmetric encryption has a different role.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.365
Once two parties share a secret key,

00:00:30.365 --> 00:00:33.156
symmetric algorithms can protect bulk data efficiently.

00:00:33.156 --> 00:00:37.240
In TLS, for example, public-key mechanisms help set up the protected session,

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.860
while symmetric encryption protects application data after the handshake.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.735
Hashing and message authentication also have important roles.

00:00:43.735 --> 00:00:46.680
These functions are affected differently by quantum considerations,

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.920
so teams should avoid treating all cryptography as one undifferentiated category.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.820
Key exchange and digital signatures solve different problems.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.565
Key exchange or key establishment helps parties derive shared secret material.

00:00:57.565 --> 00:01:02.040
Digital signatures help verify the identity of a signer and detect modification of signed

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:02.485
data.

00:01:02.485 --> 00:01:05.135
Certificates connect public keys to identities.

00:01:05.135 --> 00:01:07.955
In a TLS or VPN setting, these roles work together.

00:01:07.955 --> 00:01:10.360
If the public-key portion becomes breakable later,

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.430
captured encrypted sessions may become more vulnerable depending on protocol details and

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.600
implementation choices.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.135
The planning message is not that encryption is useless.

00:01:18.135 --> 00:01:20.920
Strong, well-implemented encryption is still essential.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.915
The message is that long-term confidentiality depends on the assumptions behind the

00:01:24.915 --> 00:01:25.750
cryptographic system,

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.995
not just on the presence of encryption labels.

00:01:27.995 --> 00:01:31.635
Teams should identify which algorithms, protocols, libraries, keys,

00:01:31.635 --> 00:01:33.575
and certificates protect long-lived data,

00:01:33.575 --> 00:01:37.600
then plan migration using standards-based and well-tested mechanisms.
